# What may reach the Docker build context. The context is /api — this file does
# not, and cannot, reach outside it.
#
# The point is cache stability: `COPY . .` sits above the compile step, so
# anything in the context invalidates the build layer. Nothing listed here can
# affect the binary, so nothing listed here should be able to trigger a rebuild.

# Docs and tooling. None of it compiles anything.
README.md
Makefile
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
.golangci.yml

# Local build artifacts. `go build ./...` drops binaries beside their package and
# `make cover` writes a profile; both are gitignored, so they never reach CI, but
# they are in the context of anyone running `make docker-build` after a local
# build.
#
# These patterns are root-anchored on purpose. Unlike .gitignore, a bare name
# here matches only at the context root — and `server` must stay that way. As
# `**/server` it would also match cmd/server, removing the only main package and
# failing the build with a message about no Go files, nowhere near the cause.
server
bin/
*.test
*.out

# Never send a repository into a build.
#
# Unreachable today — .git sits at the repo root, outside this context — which is
# why Go's VCS stamping is already inert and the build needs no -buildvcs=false.
# If the build root ever moves, the alpine builder has no git to stamp with and
# the failure reads as `error obtaining VCS status`, which points at the
# toolchain rather than at the context. Named here so that cannot happen.
.git
.gitignore

# Deliberately NOT excluded:
#
#   go.mod, go.sum   copied explicitly for the module-cache layer
#   *_test.go        excluding them would silently break any future stage that
#                    runs tests in the image, and GitHub runners keep no layer
#                    cache between runs, so the saving would be zero
